     \item \textbf{Numerical characterization and empirical interpretation.}
     Controlled experiments assess the coefficients and their local-step dependence. On real data, minibatch allocation changes finite-tail mean displacement, and model averaging can make it a substantial component of parameter error. A post-hoc full-vector comparison finds directional agreement with the derived curvature--noise structure while retaining its magnitude and local-step discrepancies.
 \end{itemize}
+
+\paragraph{Significance and scope.}
+Our contribution is a precise characterization of stationary mean bias in
+existing federated algorithms, rather than a new optimization method.
+Its significance lies in distinguishing the effects of client averaging,
+ordinary local updates, and persistent controls beyond the shared leading
+term. Resolving the $\gamma^2$ coefficients in a homogeneous additive-noise
+model provides an explicit reference for studying which effects are already
+generated by local stochastic dynamics and which additional interactions
+arise in more general settings. The \Scaf{} characterization already
+extends to affine objective shifts through
+Proposition~\ref{prop:affine-shift}, where correction of fixed gradient
+offsets coexists with the same stochastic mean-bias mechanism.
+Extensions to unequal client curvature, partial participation, and
+state-dependent noise remain natural directions for further analysis.
 
